      "problem": "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(-3a,b)$ and $(4a,-6b)$\n is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(-3a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(4a,-6b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-3a+4a}{2},\\frac{b+(-6b)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{a}{2},\\frac{-5b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{a}{2},-\\frac{5b}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
